		"meta": {
			"author": "",
			"category": "source",
			"deploymentTarget": {
				"branch": "",
				"name": "Wire",
				"version": {
					"major": 7,
					"minor": 24,
					"patch": 0
				}
			},
			"description": "Bass Strings - four round-wound double bass strings (E A D G, left to right) that vibrate with the music, inspired by Len Lye's lines that move to the music. Each string is driven by one sound band: E = low, A = low-mid, D = mid, G = high; the strings also stand for the other instruments of the band. A vibrating string shows as a translucent band, brighter at its turning points, with the string itself swinging inside; louder bands swing wider and add overtones. Silence = straight, still strings. Sound analysis like Lye Swing v2 (exception to the base-patch rule): set Spectrum In to External. OUTPUT - Backdrop On\/Color (off = transparent), Strings Opacity, Show Meter (level bars bottom left + learned range on a 20 Hz-20 kHz axis, for tuning). STRINGS - Center X (-1..1), Spacing (share of the screen width), Line Width (E string; the others get thinner), Edge Soft, one color per string. Light Angle: light from left (negative) or right (positive), gives the round look, highlight and the shadow on the ground. Shine: highlight strength. Winding Pitch: distance of the slanted winding grooves (finer towards G). Winding Depth: how deep the grooves look. VIBRATION - Amplitude: widest swing at full level (share of the screen width). Vibe Speed: visible swing rate of the E string; the others are a fourth apart each. Overtones: extra modes at high levels (restless string). Blur: strength of the translucent swing band. Twist: second image of the string half a swing earlier, gives the rope look. Node Top \/ Node Bottom: fixed ends of the strings (-1 = bottom, 1 = top edge). Beyond the screen the whole visible string swings; inside the screen the string stays still beyond its fixed end (like on the bridge; default Node Bottom -0.15). SOUND INPUT - Overall Boost raises all bands. Gate: band levels below this stay silent, above it the string fades in softly (raise it if strings flutter on quiet noise; applies to all strings after their Boost). Loudest Note Mix: 0 = band average (calm), 1 = loudest note in the band (lively). Range Depth: how far (dB) below the loudest content still counts as used range. Range Shrink: seconds until the learned range narrows again (it widens within about a second). Min Lo\/Hi Edge: the range never gets narrower. Hz per FFT Step: 23.44 at 48 kHz. BAND WIDTHS - share of each band on a log scale across the learned range. PER BAND - Boost: sensitivity of the string, Fade: seconds to fall back (low strings ring longer). Shaders BassStringsAnalysis.fs and BassStrings.fs must stay next to the patch.",
			"displayName": "Bass Strings",
			"identifier": "9b569afb-7272-483f-a983-53bb61531805",
